# Golden (Restyle, roadmap 9320, Phase 5 Item A, docs/design/restyle-musical-
# scope.md + restyle-placement.md): the node-9320 feature itself -- the
# imported melody's OWN notes transformed into the "blues" style's idiom
# (rhythm snap-to-grid + that style's own groove::apply, register anchored
# into the kLead comping range) instead of Accompany's verbatim melody-thru.
#
# `restyle blues` selects the target style AND flips the raw melody-thru OFF
# (the double-note guard, restyle-placement.md §2) so only the RESTYLED
# notes reach the shared scheduler -- on their own fixed port (2), distinct
# from the band (port 0) and the (now-silenced) melody-thru port (1).
#
# `style load basic` seeds the followed chord to the C-major home triad
# (Engine::cmd_style's own establish_default seeding, no live chord steer
# needed here) so the classifier has a known, deterministic ChordState/Key to
# work against without exercising live chord-detect in this golden.
#
# The shared fixture `components/platform/midisrc/tests/fixtures/tiny.mid` (also used
# by accompany_basic/accompany_melody_detect and midisrc's own tests) carries
# C4 (a chord tone: the root) then D4 (a scale degree, not a chord tone -- ii
# over the C-major home chord) on channel 0, plus one drum hit (channel 9,
# note 36) on the SAME melody port. This first slice is deliberately
# CHANNEL-BLIND (restyle-musical-scope.md/-placement.md never scope a
# channel filter): the drum hit's pitch class (36 % 12 = 0, same as the
# chord root) classifies and restyles exactly like any other chord-tone
# note. That is an honest, documented characteristic of this first slice,
# not a bug -- a future slice may add channel-aware filtering.
key C major
style load basic
restyle blues
midi-source load components/platform/midisrc/tests/fixtures/tiny.mid
advance 3000
quit
